<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A CONTAINER-BACKED CLIP MUST DRAW ITS WAVEFORM AS SOON AS ITS CAPTURE EXISTS.

  `SCut::getPreview` used to bail unless getPreviewCapture() - the Preview
  ASPECT PAGE - was non-null, and that gate was wrong twice over:

    * it is not a freshness signal. getCapture() returns the current page even
      when the requested aspects are MISSING ("stale is OK; better than
      null/dropout"), so a non-null page says only that the revalidator has
      ever published one for this cut;
    * the data this function actually reads is capPeaks_, built by
      ensureCapturePeaks() from captureSnapshot() - and invalidateCapture()
      frees capPeaks_ in the SAME breath as it resets currentPage_ and
      capture_. The capture therefore carries the identical invalidation
      guarantee, applied to the data actually used.

  The cost was that a container-backed cut - an ASSET, or the SCut wrapping an
  STakeStack - painted as a SOLID BODY with no waveform whenever no page had
  been published, with a complete capture sitting right there. Measured
  headlessly after load + render + a 3 s settle: `page=0 snap=1`, with
  buildCapture_ having logged a 192000-frame container capture five times.

  NOTHING IN A HEADLESS RUN EVER PUBLISHES THAT PAGE, so before this every
  asset clip was a blank rectangle in every headless context and the
  composite lane's waveform could not be gated at all. That is what this case
  exists to hold shut: it is the first case in the suite that measures a
  container-backed clip's DRAWN MATERIAL.

  THE FIXTURE IS THE TAKE-LANE ONE, deliberately. `takestack_wrap_slipped.qxp`
  puts a 2 s wrapper window 2 s into a 4 s take over test_gapsaw.wav, whose
  EXACT-ZERO regions at [0.6,0.9) and [2.5,3.0) s make the drawn material a
  closed form: the correct window shows source [2.0,4.0), i.e. ONE gap at
  25%..50% of the clip. So this case does not merely assert "something was
  drawn" - it asserts the RIGHT audio was drawn, at the right place.

  AND IT ASSERTS THE TWO LANES AGREE. The composite lane reads the wrapper's
  CAPTURE; the take lane reads the take's own preview. Those are two different
  data paths over the same material, so "they land in the same columns" is a
  real claim and not a tautology - it was measured at bestShift 0, correlation
  0.9967, and it is the property that makes comping by eye possible.

  `<render>` is what builds the capture (via ensureReader -> buildCapture_);
  without it there is nothing for either gate to measure, and the case would
  pass vacuously on the "no capture" fallback path.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="asset_clip_preview">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../takestack_wrap_slipped.qxp"/>

    <!-- Builds the container capture the composite lane previews from. -->
    <render filename="asset_clip_preview.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="2.5"/>

    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="0"/>

    <!-- After the load and the lane change - see take_lane_domain.qxa's header
         for why a grid-disable anywhere earlier does not hold, and why the
         grid matters at all (it is drawn in EXACTLY the clip-body colour). -->
    <grid-disable/>

    <!-- THE COMPOSITE LANE (takeRow="-1"). Before the fix this was one solid
         body: gapCols 0, and firstGapStartPct -1. -->
    <assert-take-lane trackPath="0" takeRow="-1"
                      grabWidth="900" grabHeight="600"
                      grabPng="asset_clip_preview_composite.png"
                      spanFirst="1" spanLast="399" spanTolerance="2"
                      minGapCols="1" gapRuns="1"
                      firstGapStartPct="25" firstGapEndPct="50"
                      pctTolerance="3"
                      minWaveMeanPct="50" maxWaveMeanPct="75"/>

    <!-- THE ACTIVE TAKE LANE, over the same window through the other data
         path. Identical expectations: that is the agreement claim. -->
    <assert-take-lane trackPath="0" takeRow="0"
                      grabWidth="900" grabHeight="600"
                      minGapCols="1" gapRuns="1"
                      firstGapStartPct="25" firstGapEndPct="50"
                      pctTolerance="3"
                      minWaveMeanPct="50" maxWaveMeanPct="75"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
